WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.744
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.465 --> 00:00:08.340
DAN CIRULI: I want to
talk briefly about what

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.390
Istio does for you.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.720
I'm not going to get
into the componentry that

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.280
make it all
possible, but there's

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.230
three real benefits to Istio,
and the first is observability.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.537
Applications are distributed
now, they just are.

00:00:21.537 --> 00:00:23.370
Even if you're just
running your application

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:26.442
in a single Kubernetes
cluster, in all likelihood,

00:00:26.442 --> 00:00:27.900
to get more out of
your developers,

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.150
to be more productive, you've
gone to a micro services

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.420
architecture and
there's communication

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.068
between the services
and your application.

00:00:36.068 --> 00:00:37.860
When there's communication
between services

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.050
and application,
it means that you

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.510
need to understand that
communication in order

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.520
to understand your application.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.650
In order to understand why
it's performing the way

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:50.700
it's performing, in order to
make sure that the traffic is

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.110
going where you need it to.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:56.760
So Istio offers ways to gather
telemetry, to gather logs,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.460
and to gather traces
from every component

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.080
of your application,
every service,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.330
so that you can understand it.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.590
Second, it offers
operational agility.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.220
It offers without
recompiling your application,

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:11.530
without changing anything
in your source code,

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.380
the ability to change
a retry policy,

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.680
to change a backup policy,
to redirect traffic

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.890
because one of your
back ends is slow,

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:24.190
to do a canary test to make
sure that you can roll out

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:26.170
a new version easily.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.270
So it offers that
operational agility.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:31.030
And the third benefit
is security, the ability

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.820
to encrypt all of your
traffic in transit,

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:36.490
and the ability to
use policy to control

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:41.172
which services can communicate
with other services.

00:01:41.172 --> 00:01:42.880
And one thing that
we'll talk about later

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.720
but I'd like to
emphasize about Istio,

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.178
is that I consider Istio
very much an al a carte menu.

00:01:49.178 --> 00:01:50.220
It has a lot of features.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.730
It has a lot of components.

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:53.640
However, and however
daunting that

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.500
may sound at times,
one thing I like

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.980
to emphasize when we talk about
Istio is that it is a la carte.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:02.280
And you can choose the
things that you want.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.500
I talked to many
customers about Istio,

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.340
and I often say there's
no silver bullet.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:09.300
There's not one feature
that everybody needs.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.190
For some people, security.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.460
All they want is security.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.590
They want mTLS.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.230
They need encryption
between their services.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.030
They want authorization policy.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.070
That's all they need, and
Istio gives them that.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.493
Other people feel like they're
flying blind in Kubernetes,

00:02:22.493 --> 00:02:23.910
and when their app
is running slow

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.110
they don't know why
it's running slow.

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:28.985
So that observability
is the right thing.

00:02:28.985 --> 00:02:31.110
And for other people it's
that operational agility.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.568
It's that they need to be able
to do a canary test in order

00:02:33.568 --> 00:02:34.830
to release safely.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:36.573
So it is an a la
carte menu, and you

00:02:36.573 --> 00:02:38.490
can use the features
that are important to you

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:42.130
and just not use the other ones.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.340
We talk a lot about
Kubernetes with Istio,

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:49.660
and we did start
with Kubernetes.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.710
That was always where
we intended to start,

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.100
however it's not
just for Kubernetes.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.192
We do run our control
plane in Kubernetes,

00:02:56.192 --> 00:02:58.150
and there's some things
that are nice about it.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:59.710
And the two things
in particular that

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.360
were nice about Kubernetes,
well, three things.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:07.360
One is Kubernetes allowed us to
deploy a sidecar proxy easily

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.610
and transparently
without engineers

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:12.700
having to redo anything about
the way they do deployment.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.760
Having that sidecar lets us
do all the magic of Istio.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.195
Second, Kubernetes offers
a service registry.

00:03:18.195 --> 00:03:19.820
So we know where
everything is running,

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:21.528
and when something
scales up we know that

00:03:21.528 --> 00:03:24.280
and we can direct
traffic appropriately.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.955
And thirdly, with
that sidecar proxy

00:03:26.955 --> 00:03:28.330
it allows us to
do the networking

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.880
we need to make sure that
all the traffic into and out

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:32.720
of the service goes through it.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.690
However, those problems are
solvable outside of Kubernetes,

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:36.190
also.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:38.470
And we're working
with customers today

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:43.480
who are extending their service
measures by installing proxies

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:44.820
in VM based workloads.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:46.570
And we even have
customers who are looking

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:51.520
at how they will do that to
even more legacy things, things

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.597
that are running in mainframes.

00:03:55.597 --> 00:03:57.180
Another thing, the
service mesh if you

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.610
look at the Google Trends
graph on service mesh

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:03.150
it has certainly gone up a lot
in the last couple of years.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:04.870
A lot of service
meshes are out there.

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:06.750
All of them are open source.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.590
The thing that distinguishes
Istio more than anything else

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.120
is that this is the
one service mesh

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:15.102
project that a lot of the
industry is getting behind.

00:04:15.102 --> 00:04:17.019
There are other service
measures each of which

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:20.180
is built by a company.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:23.910
We started this with IBM,
we work very, very closely

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.530
with Lyft and
they're envoy proxy,

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:27.390
but Red Hat, VMware,
Cisco, and the list

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.220
goes on and on
behind companies who

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.560
have realized that
just like it's happened

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.930
with orchestration
and Kubernetes,

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:36.810
it will be better for our users
if we can all work together

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:37.790
on one technology.

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:40.140
And so this is the
service mesh that

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:42.660
has the most companies
behind it and has the most

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.883
momentum because of that.

00:04:44.883 --> 00:04:46.550
And with that, I'm
going to turn it over

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:51.060
to Russ, who will tell you a
little bit about Autotrader.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.630
RUSSELL WARMAN: Thanks Dan.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.560
So we're based in the UK.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.800
We're the UK version
of Autotrader.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.420
And we are the UK's largest
automotive marketplace,

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.790
and our goal is about
leading the digital future

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:07.540
of that marketplace.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.960
We're the 16th
biggest UK website,

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:11.910
and we've got something
in the region of 55

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:14.650
million cross platform visits.

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:16.290
And on average
we've got probably

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:18.420
around 450,000
vehicles advertised

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:21.220
on our platform, our
marketplace, each day.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:25.680
And as you can see, we've got
around four times more auto

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:28.313
searches than Google have
on their platform, which

00:05:28.313 --> 00:05:28.980
is a good start.

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:33.960
We've been around
for about 40 years.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.800
And we've seen lots of
transformation through there.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:40.010
We successfully migrated
all our revenues

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:44.090
from our print business
to digital back in 2013,

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.640
where we stopped
producing magazines.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.800
And Karl and I were chatting
about this yesterday,

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.730
and we feel that we're almost
in the next phase of sort

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:55.610
of migration in terms
of becoming more cloud.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:58.700
We've been building our private
cloud for probably six or seven

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.568
years, but we're now
in a sort of next stage

00:06:00.568 --> 00:06:02.360
of that transformation
where we're starting

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:03.527
to take more cloud services.

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:09.330
We are a very values
driven organization.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:14.410
We use a lot of data to
inform our decision making.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:16.580
We like experiment
and then failing fast,

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:18.850
and we use technology
to enable us to do that.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:22.370
And we try and avoid using it
because it's shiny and cool.

00:06:26.142 --> 00:06:27.600
DAN CIRULI: One
thing I will say is

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.670
that the transformation
that's happened

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:34.530
in our industries over the
last decade, decade and a half

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:38.910
as industry after industry has
been changed by technology,

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:42.492
we've seen very large companies
get put out of business.

00:06:42.492 --> 00:06:43.950
What's interesting
about Autotrader

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:47.280
and gives you a little insight
into how they view innovation

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.868
is that they didn't get put
out of business by someone

00:06:49.868 --> 00:06:51.660
coming in and taking
your market digitally.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:55.380
They realized what was happening
and with a very innovation

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:58.870
embracing culture have
established themselves

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:01.320
as they were a leader then,
and they're a leader now.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.293
So I say this just
to say that they're

00:07:03.293 --> 00:07:04.710
fostering a culture
of innovation,

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:05.835
and it's really paying off.

00:07:05.835 --> 00:07:08.430
In fact, you might say it
kept in business, right?

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:12.690
So let's talk about where
you were a year or so ago

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:14.460
before you started
investigating Istio,

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:18.880
and what the situation was like
and why you got led into this.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:21.060
So first, let's just
talk practically.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:24.600
How big is your team, how
many developers do you have,

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.950
and how many
services do you have?

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:28.450
KARL STONEY: OK,
so great questions.

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:30.360
The number of services
that we have, like I'm sure

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.410
a lot of your organizations,
is constantly in flux.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.410
We have new ones coming online
every day and going away,

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:36.000
but at any given
point in time it

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.380
floats between about
350 and 400 services.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:42.380
It's a very micro
service architecture.

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:45.102
So if you think about
our main website

00:07:45.102 --> 00:07:47.310
it's probably the easiest
way to visualize this path.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:50.592
If you go in search for a car
on that you might hit seven,

00:07:50.592 --> 00:07:52.800
eight, maybe nine different
services in the back end.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.518
So we have a whole mesh
of things pieced together.

00:07:57.518 --> 00:07:58.935
In terms of actual
infrastructure,

00:07:58.935 --> 00:08:00.643
because I know there
will be a few of you

00:08:00.643 --> 00:08:03.060
in here interested in that,
we have two big physical data

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:06.600
centers based over in
Manchester in the UK.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.300
In those data centers we have
what we consider our oldest

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:11.940
platform to be,
which was a VMware

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:15.570
stack with servers that were
built using Chef and Puppet.

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:17.910
We have a tool which deploys
code onto those servers

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.970
but they were long live servers.

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:23.430
The next evolution
of that platform.

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:26.350
was our private cloud, which
Dan touched on a minute ago,

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:27.750
which was Phoenix based servers.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:29.340
So every time we
did a deployment,

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:31.523
we got new servers as well.

00:08:31.523 --> 00:08:32.940
So we're moving
in that direction.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:36.914
That private cloud brings in
some standardization as well.

00:08:36.914 --> 00:08:38.789
One of the problems that
we've got on premise

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:42.570
is all instances
of our applications

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:45.517
had two CPUs and
two gigs of RAM,

00:08:45.517 --> 00:08:47.350
regardless of whether
or not they needed it.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:48.720
So you might have
like a little node web

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:50.550
service which needs a
tenth of that but it

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:54.840
was just a standard amount
of CPU and RAM given.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:58.810
In terms of developers
we've got around about 200,

00:08:58.810 --> 00:08:59.550
would you say?

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.080
Around about 200 developers.

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:03.330
But we do have a relatively
centralized infrastructure

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:06.450
and operations team
that I'm part of,

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:08.720
which is a grand total
of about 20 people.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:12.180
So you can see the ratio
of operations support

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:13.485
against developers.

00:09:13.485 --> 00:09:14.860
DAN CIRULI: Yeah,
so that's good.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:18.780
So you had several hundred
services, a couple hundred

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:22.320
developers, and a small
infrastructure and operations

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:22.820
team each.

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:25.920
10 or so, each of those, great.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.420
And you mentioned cloud.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:32.385
So you essentially built your
own private cloud, right?

00:09:32.385 --> 00:09:33.510
RUSSELL WARMAN: Yes, right.

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.300
DAN CIRULI: And what
led you to do that?

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:38.670
RUSSELL WARMAN: We've moved
over the last six or seven

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:41.350
years from manual
deployments into auto deploy,

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:45.690
and we wanted to make sure
that as an application was

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:47.772
released we were releasing
the infrastructure

00:09:47.772 --> 00:09:48.480
at the same time.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:51.510
So getting more towards the
sort of Phoenix servers Karl

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:52.520
talked on before.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:55.547
DAN CIRULI: And was it that
increased rate of deployment?

00:09:55.547 --> 00:09:57.130
Is that what you
wanted, more agility?

00:09:57.130 --> 00:09:58.505
RUSSELL WARMAN:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:09:58.505 --> 00:10:00.880
We wanted to put the control
back in the developers hands

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:03.930
so they're not looking at the
infrastructure and operations

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:06.370
teams to be able to
deploy their applications.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:08.610
We wanted them to be able
to do that themselves.

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:12.190
Smaller features, more
frequent releases,

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:13.570
that was the ambition.

00:10:13.570 --> 00:10:15.153
DAN CIRULI: So no
one has to call Karl

00:10:15.153 --> 00:10:17.820
and say spin me up a VM, I'm
going to install something.

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:18.990
Was it a Git based workflow?

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:19.610
RUSSELL WARMAN: Exactly.

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:20.235
DAN CIRULI: OK.

00:10:20.235 --> 00:10:21.120
OK, great.

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.422
And how long did
a deployment take?

00:10:23.422 --> 00:10:25.380
RUSSELL WARMAN: For some
of the older stuff, so

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:27.780
on the virtualization or
traditional virtualization

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:29.910
platform, it could
take a few hours.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:32.435
For the private cloud it was
something like 10 minutes.

00:10:32.435 --> 00:10:33.060
DAN CIRULI: OK.

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:34.690
RUSSELL WARMAN: And that
was with no manual work.

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:35.315
DAN CIRULI: OK.

00:10:35.315 --> 00:10:37.530
So you had undergone a
transformation on your own.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:41.290
You had you had seen the
need to do this by yourself.

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:43.440
OK, so now you've got a
couple hundred developers,

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.390
you have 10 people
in operations.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:47.297
How did they know
what was going on?

00:10:47.297 --> 00:10:49.380
RUSSELL WARMAN: So the
operations team pretty much

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:51.540
look after all the
monitoring for customer

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:54.560
facing applications, and
they're all done the same.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:56.880
But the individual
micro services

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:59.430
are all monitored
differently, and the teams

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:02.375
had different logs and wrote
their own metrics for those.

00:11:02.375 --> 00:11:04.250
And probably you can
explain a little bit on.

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:06.300
KARL STONEY: Yeah, sure.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.280
So if you think
back to the graph

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:10.380
that you saw a minute ago
about our organization

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:12.270
and the transformation,
it's gone on

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.640
from a magazine business to
a fully digital business.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.020
What happens generally
within those sorts of growths

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:20.970
is you get organic
growth of tooling.

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:23.070
So different teams pop
up different tools.

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:25.020
They're all trying to
solve similar problems.

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:27.270
What you end up with is
we've got the elastic stack,

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.090
we've got Graphite, we've
got Grafana, we've got Sensu,

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:32.280
we use SolarWinds.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:34.800
The list goes on because all
of these teams, fundamentally

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:36.258
what they want to
be able to see is

00:11:36.258 --> 00:11:38.352
the health of their
application, or applications

00:11:38.352 --> 00:11:39.060
as I said before.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:42.300
Seven, eight, nine services
to service that customer's

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:43.130
request.

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:46.780
They might be monitored in
three or four different ways.

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:50.025
So if you can think about
the operation teams problem

00:11:50.025 --> 00:11:52.150
that they're getting an
alert at two in the morning

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:54.090
that the customer
facing websites

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:57.150
experiencing a high error rate,
trying to actually track down

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:00.510
the root cause of that
problem is super difficult.

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:03.640
DAN CIRULI: So different
operational tools,

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:05.740
different monitoring tools
for different services.

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:07.490
Yeah, I can see the
life of the ops person

00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:09.580
would be pretty rough.

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:11.275
And what about security?

00:12:11.275 --> 00:12:13.650
You do a lot of traffic between
these services obviously.

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:15.515
Any encryption on that?

00:12:15.515 --> 00:12:16.890
KARL STONEY: No,
so actually this

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:18.700
is one of the main
drivers for Istio.

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:20.658
I know we're going to
touch on it a little bit,

00:12:20.658 --> 00:12:24.450
but a lot of you
all know we do HTTPS

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:27.390
to the edge of our
infrastructure.

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:29.880
But the communication between
the services internally,

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:32.460
with inside the same area
of the network effectively,

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:34.920
was just HTTP with
AuthN and AuthC,

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:36.932
but it wasn't
encrypted in transit.

00:12:36.932 --> 00:12:38.640
That was one of the
things that we really

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.337
wanted to tackle as well.

00:12:40.337 --> 00:12:42.420
DAN CIRULI: What kind of
languages were you using?

00:12:46.255 --> 00:12:49.440
KARL STONEY: I mean, we're
typically a Java shop.

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:51.798
On the really, really old
stuff, we've got a mixture.

00:12:51.798 --> 00:12:52.590
We got some Python.

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:54.890
We've got some stuff
running on Windows.

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:58.770
But I'd probably say 90% Java
on the private cloud, which

00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:01.977
is sort of middle evolution
of our platform, the one that

00:13:01.977 --> 00:13:04.060
got the deployment times
down to about 10 minutes.

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:05.602
One of the ways that
we achieved that

00:13:05.602 --> 00:13:09.040
was enforcing quite a
bit more standardization.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:11.340
So the deployable
artifact for that platform

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:12.870
was a JAR, effectively.

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:15.300
So it was Java or
nothing on that platform.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:16.110
DAN CIRULI: Right.

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:18.088
So developers would
check in the code,

00:13:18.088 --> 00:13:20.130
and you had a pipeline
they would take that code,

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:23.440
generate a JAR, spin up a VM,
deploy that JAR to the VM,

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:23.940
right?

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:25.000
KARL STONEY: Yeah exactly that.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:27.333
It would check it in, build
a JAR, post it to a service,

00:13:27.333 --> 00:13:28.550
and it gets published out.

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:30.630
DAN CIRULI: Was Java right
for all the use cases?

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:32.000
KARL STONEY: Absolutely not.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:34.140
But the thing that
developers will

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:37.100
do when faced with a
problem or a constraint,

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:38.590
they will work around it.

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:41.640
So what we find was happening
was that people, for example,

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:44.520
they were wanting to
run a simple single page

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:47.582
application that they've
written in Express, in Node,

00:13:47.582 --> 00:13:48.540
or something like that.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:51.060
They would embed
this Node service

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:53.790
into a Java build
process and into a JAR

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:56.092
just so that they could
deploy it onto the platform

00:13:56.092 --> 00:13:58.050
because they want the
benefits of the platform.

00:13:58.050 --> 00:13:59.842
They want the
standardization and they want

00:13:59.842 --> 00:14:01.260
the quick deployment times.

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:03.000
The fact that they
have to use Java,

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:06.040
for them, it was a constraint
that was just causing pain.

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:07.770
DAN CIRULI: So it's
the Henry Ford rule.

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:11.440
Any language you want,
as long if it's Java.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:14.860
OK, you already hinted at
this, but you've already

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:15.940
made some great strides.

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:18.160
Why did you start using Istio?

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:20.620
What made you want to do
this, or him want to do this.

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:23.300
Who's responsible for this?

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.600
RUSSELL WARMAN: So we had
a customer requirement

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:28.580
for a new service that
we were tendering for,

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:32.330
and they asked us to provide
end to end encryption.

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:34.860
And we tried to build it
into our new platform,

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:38.430
and we started a two week spike.

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:42.640
And then after about several
months worth of effort,

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:45.520
this guy gets super
frustrated and he just said,

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:46.520
I've had enough of this.

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:48.440
I think I can do
this a different way.

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:52.250
Can I try running it with Istio?

00:14:52.250 --> 00:14:55.380
So I said OK, and then
two or three days later I

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:58.480
think he'd spun something up
and was able to demonstrate just

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:01.180
how easy it was to do
the end to end encryption

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:04.360
using Istio rather than trying
to build in the sidecar proxy

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:05.410
with our other platform.

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:07.420
DAN CIRULI: And you said
he spun something up.

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:08.990
Where was that?

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:11.590
KARL STONEY: I'll jump in.

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:14.200
Before I answer that question,
what we're actually doing

00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:15.960
is running two parallel
streams of work.

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:18.460
So as Russ said, we were trying
to implement a sidecar model

00:15:18.460 --> 00:15:20.210
onto our existing platform.

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:22.460
So when you've got a
standardized delivery platform,

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:25.510
which is that middle
one with the single JAR,

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:27.127
it's easier to add
something to that.

00:15:27.127 --> 00:15:29.710
I wouldn't say easier though,
it still became a several months

00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:32.760
process and we still weren't
successfully rolling it out.

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:35.460
The older platform, which
was a whole bunch of stuff

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:38.260
on Windows, et cetera, we just
we couldn't even do it there.

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:41.400
So just wanted to
call out that pain.

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:43.400
I couldn't see a light
at the end of the tunnel,

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:46.130
and that worried me,
which is kind of why

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:47.555
we started looking at Istio.

00:15:47.555 --> 00:15:49.930
But I wanted to prove out that
Istio was going to give us

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:52.030
what we needed to do quickly.

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:53.942
I wanted to spike
it, effectively.

00:15:53.942 --> 00:15:55.900
The quickest way for us
to do that at the time,

00:15:55.900 --> 00:15:59.140
because we were involved in
Istio at 0.2 or something

00:15:59.140 --> 00:16:02.890
back in the day, it was
to do on Kubernetes.

00:16:02.890 --> 00:16:05.380
We didn't have
Kubernetes on premise,

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:07.700
so the quickest way for
me to spin up Kubernetes

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:11.020
without to worry about
managing stuff was on GKE.

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:14.740
So that's how we managed to
basically test out and prove

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:19.120
mutual TLS, on Istio, on GKE,
in the space of a week when

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:23.130
we've been battling with manual
implementation for months.

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:24.250
DAN CIRULI: OK.

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:27.340
So to sum up where you were,
you had somewhere between 300

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:28.540
and 400 micro services.

00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:32.465
You had a couple hundred
developers, 10 people in ops,

00:16:32.465 --> 00:16:34.090
no encryption between
services, and you

00:16:34.090 --> 00:16:36.640
needed encryption all the way.

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:40.540
You had wasted some time
building mTLS yourself.

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:43.090
VM-based, a couple CPUs
to everything, no matter

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:44.680
how big it was.

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:46.310
Using JAR for
essentially everything.

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:47.500
So that's where you were.

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:49.870
You did a POC on
Istio, you found out

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:51.650
this can solve your problem.

00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:55.960
And what's interesting here is
that what you needed was mTLS.

00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:58.660
Implementing Istio on Kubernetes
was just the fastest way

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:00.110
to get that to happen.

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:02.140
And the fastest way to
get Kubernetes to happen

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:04.540
was to use GKE, right?

00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:09.520
OK, you prove that it happened,
let's talk about the decisions

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:10.540
you made.

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:12.670
From that point you call
him up and you say Russ,

00:17:12.670 --> 00:17:13.700
I have good news.

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:15.950
I can get this to work, and
I can get it to work fast.

00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:17.740
You had to make some decisions.

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:21.220
Which features out of
Istio did you need?

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:23.740
KARL STONEY: So the
Istio implementation

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:25.720
was driven out of the
requirement for mTLS

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:26.740
as we've already said.

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:29.158
So that was the first thing
we wanted to prove out,

00:17:29.158 --> 00:17:30.200
which we did very easily.

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:31.840
It was a case of turning it on.

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:34.340
We didn't have to
think about it.

00:17:34.340 --> 00:17:37.390
However, what we were also doing
because we were running on GKE

00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:38.890
and we were going
to public cloud,

00:17:38.890 --> 00:17:40.620
we were bundling in,
not just deploying

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:42.370
Istio, but effectively
a private cloud

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:44.330
to public cloud migration.

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:46.282
That's a lot of change.

00:17:46.282 --> 00:17:47.740
As I mentioned
earlier on we didn't

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:50.350
have a great amount of
insight into applications

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:51.880
from an observability
perspective

00:17:51.880 --> 00:17:53.797
on the current stack,
and we had a whole bunch

00:17:53.797 --> 00:17:54.830
of different tools.

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:57.220
So if we're moving things from
on premise to public cloud

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:58.580
and we're implementing
Istio and we're

00:17:58.580 --> 00:17:59.840
sticking it into containers.

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:02.020
How do we know
it's even working?

00:18:02.020 --> 00:18:03.820
So that was where the
uniform observability

00:18:03.820 --> 00:18:05.980
started to pull in as well as.

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:08.235
We need to be able to
observe these applications

00:18:08.235 --> 00:18:09.610
and these collection
of services,

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:12.440
and we decided to have a like
a multi cloud model as well.

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:14.650
So some stuff on premise,
some stuff in Google,

00:18:14.650 --> 00:18:16.120
we've got stuff in Amazon.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:17.890
We're all over the place.

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:19.840
We needed a consistent
view to know

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:21.940
that as we move these
things, we don't break them.

00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:24.660
So they were the two
real features for us.

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:27.233
DAN CIRULI: And the advanced
networking wasn't a driver.

00:18:27.233 --> 00:18:29.650
In fact, I don't think you've
implemented anything really.

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:31.210
KARL STONEY: We're not
really using it, no.

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:32.230
It's there in the
back of our minds

00:18:32.230 --> 00:18:34.240
is a feature that we
know we might do one day,

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:36.630
but it's a great
example of where we just

00:18:36.630 --> 00:18:37.810
didn't need to right now.

00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:40.000
DAN CIRULI: That's great, fine.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:41.530
And so you move to containers.

00:18:41.530 --> 00:18:43.750
You said something
earlier, the JAR

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:46.200
was your unit of deployment.

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:48.230
What's your unit deployment now?

00:18:48.230 --> 00:18:49.595
KARL STONEY: A container.

00:18:49.595 --> 00:18:51.970
So as I've touched on already,
people were working around

00:18:51.970 --> 00:18:54.220
the single JAR problem
by just embedding stuff

00:18:54.220 --> 00:18:55.810
in really crazy
ways, and we wanted

00:18:55.810 --> 00:18:57.230
to remove that constraint.

00:18:57.230 --> 00:19:00.190
So the deployable artifact
on the latest version

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:01.030
of our platform.

00:19:01.030 --> 00:19:03.572
When I say our platform by the
way, just so everyone's clear,

00:19:03.572 --> 00:19:06.460
I mean we've built a platform
as a service like a Heroku style

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:09.490
model on top of GKE and
Istio and the public cloud

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:11.810
providers, et cetera
to give that developer

00:19:11.810 --> 00:19:14.480
that standard interface where
they can do deployments really

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:16.215
easily.

00:19:16.215 --> 00:19:17.840
So yeah, our deployable
artifact is now

00:19:17.840 --> 00:19:20.750
a container, which
is great because it

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:24.290
means these Java
apps that are just

00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:26.210
running single page
Express servers can just

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:27.740
be expressed now.

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:32.490
But we monitor them by box
in exactly the same way.

00:19:32.490 --> 00:19:35.000
DAN CIRULI: And of course
kind of implicit, as you said,

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:35.652
you moved.

00:19:35.652 --> 00:19:36.860
You're still running a cloud.

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:39.235
It felt like a cloud before
because the developers didn't

00:19:39.235 --> 00:19:42.204
touch infrastructure,
but now it's GKE.

00:19:44.930 --> 00:19:46.430
Any notes on running Kubernetes?

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:48.472
Did you really think about
installing and running

00:19:48.472 --> 00:19:51.635
Kubernetes on prem, or
was GKE an obvious choice?

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:57.950
RUSSELL WARMAN: So we tried
to run other things on prem

00:19:57.950 --> 00:19:59.840
and failed, and
decided that we'd

00:19:59.840 --> 00:20:01.550
use cloud services instead.

00:20:01.550 --> 00:20:03.140
So we had a very
brief conversation.

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:05.250
I think it lasted
about 10 minutes,

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:07.548
and we decided that we
should just use Google.

00:20:07.548 --> 00:20:09.090
It just made absolute
sense to do it.

00:20:09.090 --> 00:20:12.960
DAN CIRULI: So for example
you would run Hadoop clusters,

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:13.460
right?

00:20:13.460 --> 00:20:14.120
RUSSELL WARMAN: We tried to.

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:15.710
DAN CIRULI: Do you still
do that, or do you not?

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:16.270
RUSSELL WARMAN: Nope.

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:16.933
DAN CIRULI: OK.

00:20:16.933 --> 00:20:19.100
So you've realized that
some of these services, it's

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:20.900
just easier to have someone
else run it for you.

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:21.440
RUSSELL WARMAN: Absolutely.

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:23.240
DAN CIRULI: And Kubernetes
fell in that bucket?

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:24.115
RUSSELL WARMAN: Yeah.

00:20:24.115 --> 00:20:26.810
We don't want to manage
the complexity of upgrades

00:20:26.810 --> 00:20:29.640
and sorting all those.

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:31.903
That level just doesn't
give any benefit to us

00:20:31.903 --> 00:20:33.320
as an organization,
so we'd rather

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:34.452
take it as a money service.

00:20:34.452 --> 00:20:35.910
DAN CIRULI: So
you're the engineer,

00:20:35.910 --> 00:20:36.920
you say this is going to happen.

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:38.450
You're the head
of infrastructure.

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:40.450
You say I believe you.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:43.390
Do you have to tell anyone
above you, hey by the way

00:20:43.390 --> 00:20:45.790
as part of solving this
problem for the customer we're

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:48.650
going to start running
things in the cloud?

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:51.750
RUSSELL WARMAN: So our strategy
has been to be cloud native,

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:57.280
and we've not defined whether
it would be private or public.

00:20:57.280 --> 00:20:58.750
And when we started
demonstrating

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:00.292
some of the features
and the benefits

00:21:00.292 --> 00:21:03.310
that we got from using GKE,
it was really, really easy

00:21:03.310 --> 00:21:07.030
to justify the decision as
to why we would do that.

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:10.288
So there wasn't really
a lot of conversation.

00:21:10.288 --> 00:21:12.080
I think people just
understood the benefits

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:16.810
and saw that it was
the right thing to do.

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:18.760
DAN CIRULI: So
when I hear people

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:22.780
talk about adopting this,
people are concerned a lot

00:21:22.780 --> 00:21:23.410
with friction.

00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:25.870
You have 200 developers.

00:21:25.870 --> 00:21:29.310
Kubernetes, factoring
Istio on top of that,

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:32.700
we're talking about a lot
of YAML, a lot of CRDs.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:36.497
How much YAML do your
developers have to deal with?

00:21:36.497 --> 00:21:38.580
KARL STONEY: This is a
really interesting question

00:21:38.580 --> 00:21:40.832
because it was one
of the bigger, well,

00:21:40.832 --> 00:21:42.540
it could have been
one of the bigger pain

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:44.790
points for
implementing Kubernetes

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:46.860
and Istio, et cetera.

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:49.240
For one service to get
deployed onto our platform,

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:52.430
as I described it before there's
about 20 different YAML files

00:21:52.430 --> 00:21:53.370
that get generated.

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:55.230
So you've got all of
the Kubernetes service

00:21:55.230 --> 00:21:57.900
manifest deployments,
then you've

00:21:57.900 --> 00:21:59.320
got all your Istio components.

00:21:59.320 --> 00:22:02.430
You've got virtual services,
destination rules, side cars.

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:05.670
We automate DNS and
TLS as well, so you've

00:22:05.670 --> 00:22:09.840
got certificates,
certificate requests, Ingress

00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:10.650
configurations.

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:13.652
So there's just tons
and tons of them.

00:22:13.652 --> 00:22:16.110
And if you think about where
we were, we were in a position

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:18.360
where developers don't think
about the infrastructure,

00:22:18.360 --> 00:22:20.360
they build a JAR and
they want that JAR

00:22:20.360 --> 00:22:21.610
to run on some infrastructure.

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:24.570
So we could just
not scale them up

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:26.528
within any sort of
reasonable time frame on all

00:22:26.528 --> 00:22:28.695
of these different APIs
because that's fundamentally

00:22:28.695 --> 00:22:29.340
what they are.

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:32.773
And some of those APIs were
under very rapid change.

00:22:32.773 --> 00:22:34.440
Like I said earlier
on, we were involved

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:37.200
in Istio very, very early
doors so we didn't want

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:39.490
to push that onto the teams.

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:41.940
So your answer is a lot of YAML.

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:43.898
DAN CIRULI: But your
developers aren't

00:22:43.898 --> 00:22:44.940
writing that YAML, right?

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:45.815
KARL STONEY: Correct.

00:22:45.815 --> 00:22:47.640
So the approach
that we've gone down

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:49.807
and I've been chatting to
a few of the companies who

00:22:49.807 --> 00:22:51.560
are doing this at the moment.

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:54.390
We've abstracted them
from that concern.

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:56.250
They just provide us
with a single YAML

00:22:56.250 --> 00:22:59.830
file, which has some key
aspects about their application.

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:03.180
So it's like a minimum amount of
instances that they should run,

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:05.640
the name of the image
that we should be running,

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:07.910
some information about
the health checks.

00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:10.170
It exposes affinities
if they want

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:12.210
to run their other applications.

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:14.670
Bit of metadata about the
service, because we have

00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:16.500
a service catalog and stuff.

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:21.540
And firewall policy, so
what is going to talk to you

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:23.340
and what do you talk to.

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:26.760
And from that behind
the scenes, the platform

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:29.010
that infrastructure and
operations are responsible for

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:31.290
generates and manages
all that other YAML.

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:32.997
And that's worked
really well for us.

00:23:32.997 --> 00:23:35.580
DAN CIRULI: So they still have
they interface with this system

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:38.970
through the CICD pipeline,
and they have one config file.

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:42.750
You generate all the right
Kubernetes and Istio files.

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:45.310
I think that's a
really important point.

00:23:45.310 --> 00:23:46.620
So you had 350 services.

00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:50.330
Did you do this one day, did
you just throw them all in?

00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:51.380
How is the progression?

00:23:51.380 --> 00:23:52.338
KARL STONEY: Overnight.

00:23:52.338 --> 00:23:56.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:56.170 --> 00:23:58.380
RUSSELL WARMAN: So,
we've started cherry

00:23:58.380 --> 00:23:59.830
picking some of the services.

00:23:59.830 --> 00:24:02.150
So the things that were
already on our private cloud,

00:24:02.150 --> 00:24:05.340
they'd been built to be cloud
native, so we've moved some

00:24:05.340 --> 00:24:07.030
of those applications across.

00:24:07.030 --> 00:24:08.580
And that gave people
the confidence

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:10.200
that it was going to work.

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:11.970
And then, in parallel
with that, we

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:13.950
started moving some
of the applications,

00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:17.250
or trying to move some
applications that historically

00:24:17.250 --> 00:24:18.750
proved difficult
because they didn't

00:24:18.750 --> 00:24:21.120
conform to the single JAR.

00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:24.000
So we started working
on those in parallel

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:26.865
to try and overcome any of the
problems that we might face.

00:24:26.865 --> 00:24:28.240
DAN CIRULI: So
you cherry picked.

00:24:28.240 --> 00:24:30.010
You cherry picked
some easy ones,

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:31.802
but you cherry picked
some thorny ones too?

00:24:31.802 --> 00:24:32.677
RUSSELL WARMAN: Yeah.

00:24:32.677 --> 00:24:33.390
DAN CIRULI: OK.

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:34.410
That's a good strategy.

00:24:34.410 --> 00:24:36.660
KARL STONEY: This is just
to add something to that one

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:37.785
actually if you don't mind.

00:24:37.785 --> 00:24:40.380
So just to give you an idea
about some of the more gnarly

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:43.370
stuff, we've got a we've got
some containers now that are

00:24:43.370 --> 00:24:45.870
running .NET Core, so we had
some Windows based applications

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:46.370
before.

00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:49.440
So we take that and use one of
those difficult applications

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:51.247
to drive out capabilities
that we wanted

00:24:51.247 --> 00:24:52.830
to deliver on the
platform and that we

00:24:52.830 --> 00:24:54.240
wanted to expose from Istio.

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:57.090
So it's a really
iterative approach

00:24:57.090 --> 00:24:59.383
that difficult applications
were driving the features,

00:24:59.383 --> 00:25:01.800
rather than just trying to
build all the features up front

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:04.480
and then release the platform.

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:06.980
DAN CIRULI: So the
process was essentially

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:10.290
you did a POC, you
built out some infra,

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:12.285
you did an initial
rollout, and yeah.

00:25:12.285 --> 00:25:13.910
And this became an
iterative thing then

00:25:13.910 --> 00:25:15.660
you rolled out to a
class of applications,

00:25:15.660 --> 00:25:17.340
you found one that wouldn't fit.

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:18.990
You would add capabilities.

00:25:21.995 --> 00:25:23.370
So the implementation,
as it went

00:25:23.370 --> 00:25:25.010
to sum up the
implementation, you

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:27.720
didn't train your developers
on Istio and Kubernetes.

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:30.805
You trained your infrastructure
and ops team, right?

00:25:30.805 --> 00:25:32.680
These people do it, your
infrastructure team.

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:35.100
You had to build a
CICD pipeline that

00:25:35.100 --> 00:25:36.832
deployed the Kubernetes GKE.

00:25:36.832 --> 00:25:40.965
Your ops team now
is trained on, I

00:25:40.965 --> 00:25:42.340
don't think we
had a slide on it,

00:25:42.340 --> 00:25:44.340
but in your new monitoring
infrastructure right?

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:47.700
KARL STONEY: Yeah, so the way
that our ops team work is they

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:49.150
generally respond to those.

00:25:49.150 --> 00:25:51.040
They're first line
support in a lot of ways.

00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:52.790
So it's like an
application is misbehaving

00:25:52.790 --> 00:25:54.610
or a customers got an
impacting scenario.

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:58.620
So the symptoms that they
see on all of these platforms

00:25:58.620 --> 00:25:59.280
are the same.

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:00.810
You know, it's a
customer impacting event.

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:02.393
But what we needed
to train them up in

00:26:02.393 --> 00:26:04.680
is, well, how do I debug
that in a container world

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:07.930
where I might have
a multi-tenant host.

00:26:07.930 --> 00:26:09.800
So we ended up building tooling.

00:26:09.800 --> 00:26:11.550
I think one of them
is called Monkeynetes,

00:26:11.550 --> 00:26:14.520
which just runs on our clusters
and generally just misbehaves,

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:16.020
and triggers alerts.

00:26:16.020 --> 00:26:17.680
And the ops teams
pick those things up.

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:20.448
And they just get
practiced right.

00:26:20.448 --> 00:26:21.990
You know, an
applications misbehaving

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:23.948
and how do I look at its logs?

00:26:23.948 --> 00:26:24.990
You know, it's different.

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:26.530
It's like how do I get
onto the box, which

00:26:26.530 --> 00:26:28.447
is the most common
question, I was like, well,

00:26:28.447 --> 00:26:30.150
there isn't a box per se.

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:32.160
So you got to think about
those sorts of things.

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:34.040
DAN CIRULI: Are you
running Prometheus?

00:26:34.040 --> 00:26:35.195
KARL STONEY: Yes.

00:26:35.195 --> 00:26:37.320
I can't remember if we had
a slide on standardized.

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:38.190
DAN CIRULI: I can't remember.

00:26:38.190 --> 00:26:39.315
Do we have a slide on that?

00:26:39.315 --> 00:26:41.962
KARL STONEY: Well we can talk
about and they will skip past.

00:26:41.962 --> 00:26:43.670
DAN CIRULI: We'll make
sure we get to it.

00:26:43.670 --> 00:26:46.120
How far along are you?

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:47.835
KARL STONEY: So, yeah.

00:26:47.835 --> 00:26:49.210
What, did you
check this morning?

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:53.370
184 applications
live out of 350.

00:26:53.370 --> 00:26:56.903
And we really only start ramping
this up about six months ago.

00:26:56.903 --> 00:26:58.570
Six to nine months,
something like that.

00:26:58.570 --> 00:27:00.720
So we were well on the way.

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:02.220
DAN CIRULI: So
you're about halfway.

00:27:02.220 --> 00:27:04.762
I'm doing my math, it's rough
but you're about halfway there.

00:27:04.762 --> 00:27:07.290
And how much traffic is going
to those Kubernetes based

00:27:07.290 --> 00:27:08.162
services?

00:27:08.162 --> 00:27:10.620
KARL STONEY: When you asked me
this question the other day,

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:13.157
because obviously we did
a little bit of prep,

00:27:13.157 --> 00:27:15.240
it's a great question
because I couldn't answer it

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:17.700
on the old platform, and this
is the observability aspect.

00:27:17.700 --> 00:27:19.200
It's like if you
came to me and said

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:22.350
Karl, how much HTTP
traffic have we

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:26.315
done on our private cloud in the
last 24 hours, I'd go not sure.

00:27:26.315 --> 00:27:27.690
I could probably
go and aggregate

00:27:27.690 --> 00:27:30.410
all of the logs from all of
the applications running there

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:32.700
and come up with some value
but it would take me ages.

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:34.533
But I think I answered
this question for you

00:27:34.533 --> 00:27:37.870
by looking at the Istio
statistics in about 10 seconds.

00:27:37.870 --> 00:27:41.220
So in the last 24 hours we've
done about 80 million HTTP

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:44.400
requests, and the overall
success rate of those requests

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:47.520
was like 99.998% at the time.

00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:49.770
And that failure rate
was covered by retries,

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:51.460
there was no client
impacts in events.

00:27:51.460 --> 00:27:55.710
So I'm just able to answer
these questions in moments

00:27:55.710 --> 00:27:57.780
DAN CIRULI: That's great.

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:00.545
And security?

00:28:00.545 --> 00:28:02.170
KARL STONEY: Turn it
on, it just works.

00:28:02.170 --> 00:28:03.170
DAN CIRULI: mTLS on everything?

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:03.920
KARL STONEY: Yeah.

00:28:03.920 --> 00:28:06.690
So a few people who've been
doing Istio implementations.

00:28:06.690 --> 00:28:09.977
Some are trying to bring in
mTLS as a phased approach.

00:28:09.977 --> 00:28:11.810
Some people are already
running GKE clusters

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:12.830
and they're out
in Istio and then

00:28:12.830 --> 00:28:14.870
they're trying to
bring in a bit of mTLS.

00:28:14.870 --> 00:28:17.363
We did mTLS mandated
from the offset.

00:28:17.363 --> 00:28:19.280
I do believe I made
things a little bit easier

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:22.280
for us because we weren't
having to mix and match

00:28:22.280 --> 00:28:23.220
different policies.

00:28:23.220 --> 00:28:24.035
DAN CIRULI: So you
run in strict mode?

00:28:24.035 --> 00:28:25.327
KARL STONEY: Strict mode, yeah.

00:28:25.327 --> 00:28:28.678
DAN CIRULI: And what are
you doing for authorization?

00:28:28.678 --> 00:28:31.220
KARL STONEY: We actually have
our own internal authentication

00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:33.620
and authorization
services that run

00:28:33.620 --> 00:28:34.810
across all of our platforms.

00:28:34.810 --> 00:28:37.280
So we're not leveraging any
of the Istio components there.

00:28:37.280 --> 00:28:39.170
We just continue to
use our existing stuff.

00:28:39.170 --> 00:28:39.795
DAN CIRULI: OK.

00:28:42.300 --> 00:28:44.850
So we did have a
slide on visibility.

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:46.350
So you're running
Prometheus, do you

00:28:46.350 --> 00:28:48.630
run one instance of
Prometheus and everybody

00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:51.240
sends data to it
through a Mixer adapter?

00:28:51.240 --> 00:28:54.420
KARL STONEY: Yeah, so we have
we have several instances

00:28:54.420 --> 00:28:57.270
of Prometheus, but when you
think about applications that

00:28:57.270 --> 00:28:59.430
are deployed onto the
delivery platform,

00:28:59.430 --> 00:29:01.560
so the ones that fit
the standard model,

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:04.070
there is just one
main Prometheus.

00:29:04.070 --> 00:29:06.570
The interesting thing that we've
done though with Prometheus

00:29:06.570 --> 00:29:08.932
is we've got all of the
Istio metrics going into it.

00:29:08.932 --> 00:29:10.890
We've got the Kubernetes
metrics going into it.

00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:14.102
But we've also standardized
other disciplines

00:29:14.102 --> 00:29:16.560
within the business, such as
database engineers, et cetera.

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:18.490
They're all sending
Prometheus metrics in now.

00:29:18.490 --> 00:29:19.948
So we actually have
a single source

00:29:19.948 --> 00:29:21.600
of truth for the
state of our platform

00:29:21.600 --> 00:29:23.252
from a lot of
different perspectives.

00:29:23.252 --> 00:29:24.960
So if you remember
that big list of stuff

00:29:24.960 --> 00:29:28.150
that I read out before
on the new platform,

00:29:28.150 --> 00:29:29.567
it's just two tools.

00:29:29.567 --> 00:29:31.650
We've built a couple of
additional alerting things

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:32.320
on top of that.

00:29:32.320 --> 00:29:34.980
So we've got like a
chat ops interface

00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:37.710
or when Istio detects a
higher than normal error

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:40.020
rate for an application
over the last 20 minutes

00:29:40.020 --> 00:29:41.490
people can own
that, wheeling that

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:42.820
through to Runbox, et cetera.

00:29:42.820 --> 00:29:45.960
So we've got a much more
consolidated view of the world

00:29:45.960 --> 00:29:46.680
now.

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:49.440
DAN CIRULI: Has it made the life
of the operations team better?

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:50.590
Are they happier people?

00:29:50.590 --> 00:29:52.380
KARL STONEY: They're not here
but I'll speak on their behalf.

00:29:52.380 --> 00:29:54.000
I think they'd be
saying definitely.

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:54.690
DAN CIRULI: OK.

00:29:54.690 --> 00:29:55.648
KARL STONEY: Great job.

00:29:55.648 --> 00:29:57.120
DAN CIRULI: That's good to know.

00:29:57.120 --> 00:30:00.110
And that Prometheus, is it
running in the same Kubernetes

00:30:00.110 --> 00:30:00.848
cluster?

00:30:00.848 --> 00:30:01.890
KARL STONEY: It is, yeah.

00:30:01.890 --> 00:30:04.120
Just for no real reason
though, to be honest.

00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:05.700
DAN CIRULI: Right.

00:30:05.700 --> 00:30:07.385
You run one big cluster?

00:30:07.385 --> 00:30:08.760
KARL STONEY: Yes,
we do actually.

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:10.135
That's another
interesting point.

00:30:10.135 --> 00:30:12.570
So I know a lot of people
doing smaller clusters.

00:30:12.570 --> 00:30:14.860
So we have one large
cluster per environment.

00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:18.047
So we have testing
dev, pre-prod.

00:30:18.047 --> 00:30:20.130
Everything in all of those
things gets replicated.

00:30:20.130 --> 00:30:23.550
So separate GCP
projects, separate VPCs,

00:30:23.550 --> 00:30:26.940
separate Kubernetes cluster,
separate firewall rules,

00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:27.440
everything.

00:30:27.440 --> 00:30:28.607
So it's all promoted across.

00:30:28.607 --> 00:30:30.658
So yeah, big cluster
per environment.

00:30:30.658 --> 00:30:31.950
We do have some other clusters.

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:35.790
We have CICD style
clusters which can access

00:30:35.790 --> 00:30:37.200
all of those environments.

00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:39.600
And we run an
elastic stack on GKE

00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:41.840
as well, that's a
separate cluster.

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:43.010
But largely, yeah.

00:30:43.010 --> 00:30:43.950
DAN CIRULI: OK.

00:30:43.950 --> 00:30:45.840
Can you talk about how
big that cluster is?

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:46.965
Do you know how many nodes?

00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:51.730
KARL STONEY: I think it was
about 400 CPUs, 800 gig of RAM

00:30:51.730 --> 00:30:53.938
per environment at the
moment, of the top of my head.

00:30:53.938 --> 00:30:56.030
DAN CIRULI: OK.

00:30:56.030 --> 00:30:58.230
And we talked a little
bit about this before,

00:30:58.230 --> 00:31:01.642
but obviously now you can put
whatever you want in that JAR.

00:31:01.642 --> 00:31:02.850
The JAR is not a JAR anymore.

00:31:02.850 --> 00:31:05.350
It's a container and you can
fill it with whatever you want.

00:31:05.350 --> 00:31:06.910
Do you find people doing that?

00:31:06.910 --> 00:31:07.910
KARL STONEY: Absolutely.

00:31:07.910 --> 00:31:12.510
So if you think about the build
time and also the startup time.

00:31:12.510 --> 00:31:14.830
I don't know how many of
you work with Java in here,

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:17.450
it can be a little bit
slower than just a node up

00:31:17.450 --> 00:31:19.250
which just has a one
module, effectively.

00:31:19.250 --> 00:31:21.180
And then it starts
almost instantaneously.

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:24.768
So developer teams,
they are switching out

00:31:24.768 --> 00:31:26.810
these big JARs where they
don't need them anymore

00:31:26.810 --> 00:31:28.685
because they get a faster
deployment process.

00:31:28.685 --> 00:31:29.900
They get quicker start times.

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:31.970
We've had a couple
of product teams

00:31:31.970 --> 00:31:34.280
that have been able to take
advantage of technologies

00:31:34.280 --> 00:31:36.280
that they wouldn't have
been able to use before,

00:31:36.280 --> 00:31:38.120
like GraphQL for example.

00:31:38.120 --> 00:31:39.500
It's not written in Java.

00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:41.210
So they can suddenly
deploy these things

00:31:41.210 --> 00:31:44.920
and get ideas into production
and test things out

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:46.570
at an extremely low cost.

00:31:46.570 --> 00:31:47.320
DAN CIRULI: Right.

00:31:47.320 --> 00:31:48.320
KARL STONEY: And in
fact test things out

00:31:48.320 --> 00:31:50.420
that they just
couldn't test before.

00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:51.950
DAN CIRULI: That's fantastic.

00:31:51.950 --> 00:31:54.117
RUSSELL WARMAN: Sorry, I'd
just like to add to that.

00:31:54.117 --> 00:31:57.110
We were doing a
migration a few weeks ago

00:31:57.110 --> 00:32:02.240
and it was for our launch
of our new price packages,

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:05.300
and partway through we
communicated to about 500

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:08.980
of our customers that we
were doing this change,

00:32:08.980 --> 00:32:10.568
and we hit a little bug.

00:32:10.568 --> 00:32:12.360
And it was about 4
o'clock in the afternoon

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:14.527
and I think it took you
about two hours or something

00:32:14.527 --> 00:32:16.520
to write and deploy an
application to fix that.

00:32:16.520 --> 00:32:18.895
That meant that we didn't have
to go back and communicate

00:32:18.895 --> 00:32:21.230
to 500 of our customers
that we were going to have

00:32:21.230 --> 00:32:22.830
to back out our release.

00:32:22.830 --> 00:32:24.860
So it's just that
these capabilities just

00:32:24.860 --> 00:32:26.340
weren't there before.

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:27.340
KARL STONEY: [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:27.340 --> 00:32:28.465
RUSSELL WARMAN: Absolutely.

00:32:28.465 --> 00:32:31.730
DAN CIRULI: That's fantastic.

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:35.000
So you talked about visibility
into your service traffic

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:38.320
and which services are
talking to which, et cetera.

00:32:38.320 --> 00:32:42.020
What the requests per second
is on any given service.

00:32:42.020 --> 00:32:45.460
But you also have visibility
into resource consumption now.

00:32:45.460 --> 00:32:48.675
So talk about how that has
changed your efficiency.

00:32:51.350 --> 00:32:53.480
RUSSELL WARMAN: So as
Karl said, everything

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:57.790
used to be provisioned with
two CPU's and two gig of RAM.

00:32:57.790 --> 00:32:59.540
Now what we're seeing
through the platform

00:32:59.540 --> 00:33:02.630
is that we've just got greater
visibility of how much resource

00:33:02.630 --> 00:33:04.430
it's actually needing.

00:33:04.430 --> 00:33:07.910
So we'd migrate applications
up exactly as they

00:33:07.910 --> 00:33:09.478
were and then rightsize them.

00:33:09.478 --> 00:33:11.270
And in a lot of instances,
we were probably

00:33:11.270 --> 00:33:12.830
reducing it by about half.

00:33:12.830 --> 00:33:14.600
And increasingly
what we're stating

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:18.830
is we'd probably need about 60%
to 70% less resources in GKE

00:33:18.830 --> 00:33:20.790
than we did on prem.

00:33:20.790 --> 00:33:24.690
So it's about 0.3 of a
core, around a gig of RAM,

00:33:24.690 --> 00:33:28.950
and some of those then
will be changed accordingly

00:33:28.950 --> 00:33:30.620
DAN CIRULI: OK.

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:32.342
Who makes sure that
a given service has

00:33:32.342 --> 00:33:33.550
the right amount of resource?

00:33:33.550 --> 00:33:35.053
Is the ops team doing that?

00:33:35.053 --> 00:33:35.720
KARL STONEY: No.

00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:38.345
So one of the changes that these
platforms actually brought in,

00:33:38.345 --> 00:33:40.520
so if you remember before
they just published a JAR.

00:33:40.520 --> 00:33:42.710
Now they're responsible
for the Docker file

00:33:42.710 --> 00:33:45.470
and also that manifest file.

00:33:45.470 --> 00:33:48.950
The CPU and RAM there is
editable by the product teams.

00:33:48.950 --> 00:33:50.650
It's checked into
their Git repo.

00:33:50.650 --> 00:33:53.370
It's also editable by the ops
engineers or the infrastructure

00:33:53.370 --> 00:33:53.870
engineers.

00:33:53.870 --> 00:33:56.180
Anybody can check into
that repo effectively

00:33:56.180 --> 00:33:57.055
and make that change.

00:33:57.055 --> 00:33:57.555
I

00:33:57.555 --> 00:33:59.210
Think the key thing
to point out is now

00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:01.640
everybody has the visibility
in order to make an informed

00:34:01.640 --> 00:34:03.920
change, whereas before it
was a little bit shooting

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:04.730
in the dark.

00:34:04.730 --> 00:34:06.110
So what we're
increasingly seeing

00:34:06.110 --> 00:34:10.670
is operations teams and the
development teams sitting

00:34:10.670 --> 00:34:12.409
together looking at
the graphs, looking

00:34:12.409 --> 00:34:13.760
at the performance
of the application,

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:15.302
looking at infrastructure
statistics,

00:34:15.302 --> 00:34:16.847
and making those
changes together.

00:34:16.847 --> 00:34:18.889
Whereas before it used to
be very over the fence,

00:34:18.889 --> 00:34:23.205
so it's actually pushing a more
dev ops model which is great.

00:34:23.205 --> 00:34:24.080
DAN CIRULI: And cost.

00:34:24.080 --> 00:34:27.770
So you're renting space
in a data center, right?

00:34:27.770 --> 00:34:30.870
Now you're writing us
a check every month.

00:34:30.870 --> 00:34:33.830
There are all kinds of
implications for cost.

00:34:33.830 --> 00:34:36.585
How does your boss
think about that?

00:34:36.585 --> 00:34:38.210
RUSSELL WARMAN: I
guess ultimately what

00:34:38.210 --> 00:34:40.760
they're looking for is
some confidence that we've

00:34:40.760 --> 00:34:43.580
got a plan to move out
of our data centers,

00:34:43.580 --> 00:34:45.560
consolidate our
workloads in the cloud.

00:34:45.560 --> 00:34:49.370
And we're being quite
aggressive in that migration.

00:34:49.370 --> 00:34:52.550
So we have an ambition to
do it in 18 months and then

00:34:52.550 --> 00:34:54.199
once we've done
that, we'll start

00:34:54.199 --> 00:34:56.760
looking to reduce our footprint
on physical data centers.

00:34:56.760 --> 00:34:59.177
DAN CIRULI: So you'll start
renting less space as you can.

00:35:01.910 --> 00:35:09.590
What's interesting here is that
your cost was not your driver

00:35:09.590 --> 00:35:10.230
for doing this.

00:35:10.230 --> 00:35:12.420
Your reason for doing
this was mTLS, ultimately.

00:35:12.420 --> 00:35:13.100
RUSSELL WARMAN: Absolutely.

00:35:13.100 --> 00:35:14.660
DAN CIRULI: In the
end, do you think

00:35:14.660 --> 00:35:16.710
that because of the
reduction in footprint

00:35:16.710 --> 00:35:17.793
is it going to be cheaper?

00:35:17.793 --> 00:35:20.085
RUSSELL WARMAN: I'm not making
any commitments on that.

00:35:20.085 --> 00:35:21.100
DAN CIRULI: OK.

00:35:21.100 --> 00:35:24.132
So that's interesting, but
the innovation is good, right?

00:35:24.132 --> 00:35:25.090
RUSSELL WARMAN: I think

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:26.250
DAN CIRULI: It was
never driven by cost,

00:35:26.250 --> 00:35:27.250
it still won't be
driven by cost.

00:35:27.250 --> 00:35:28.667
RUSSELL WARMAN:
No, we don't think

00:35:28.667 --> 00:35:31.150
it's going to cost us any
more, but what we do know

00:35:31.150 --> 00:35:33.603
is that it will give
us greater capability

00:35:33.603 --> 00:35:35.770
than we could provision
within our own data centers.

00:35:35.770 --> 00:35:40.660
So, Karl touched on it before
in terms of operational agility.

00:35:40.660 --> 00:35:43.330
The benefits that we get
from running GKE just far

00:35:43.330 --> 00:35:45.290
outweigh way to trying
to do that on prem.

00:35:45.290 --> 00:35:46.670
DAN CIRULI: Right.

00:35:46.670 --> 00:35:49.170
KARL STONEY: I think there is
an interesting point though as

00:35:49.170 --> 00:35:51.660
well, and it's if we had
like for like infrastructure,

00:35:51.660 --> 00:35:53.750
like the same number of
applications and stuff,

00:35:53.750 --> 00:35:54.500
probably it would.

00:35:54.500 --> 00:35:56.625
But because of the new
capabilities that we've got,

00:35:56.625 --> 00:35:58.337
we're building more apps.

00:35:58.337 --> 00:36:00.920
So it's going to end up probably
costing us a little bit more,

00:36:00.920 --> 00:36:03.180
but that's because we're
delivering more capabilities.

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:05.360
DAN CIRULI: So you're
also running Istio now

00:36:05.360 --> 00:36:06.365
in that cluster, right?

00:36:06.365 --> 00:36:08.740
So you're running Mixer, Pilot,
you're running a sidecar.

00:36:08.740 --> 00:36:10.370
Do you know how much
it's costing you?

00:36:10.370 --> 00:36:13.990
KARL STONEY: Yeah, so I actually
calculated this the other day.

00:36:13.990 --> 00:36:16.538
It's about CPU and RAM
utilization on our cluster.

00:36:16.538 --> 00:36:18.830
Bearing in mind we are doing
mTLS, which is heavyweight

00:36:18.830 --> 00:36:22.250
everywhere, about 5%
of our CPU and RAM

00:36:22.250 --> 00:36:24.805
is used towards the
Istio control plane.

00:36:24.805 --> 00:36:26.180
Because it's an
opex model and we

00:36:26.180 --> 00:36:28.310
know how much CPU and RAM
costs on Google Cloud,

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:31.400
we've worked out that
it costs us about $4

00:36:31.400 --> 00:36:35.840
per service, as in per
application per month

00:36:35.840 --> 00:36:37.658
to get all of those
capabilities of Istio.

00:36:37.658 --> 00:36:39.950
DAN CIRULI: So there's some
invisibility that you have.

00:36:39.950 --> 00:36:42.080
It's kind of incredible
that you can calculate that.

00:36:42.080 --> 00:36:42.890
KARL STONEY: I
could never tell you

00:36:42.890 --> 00:36:44.280
anything like that on premise.

00:36:44.280 --> 00:36:46.430
DAN CIRULI: OK,
so what do you use

00:36:46.430 --> 00:36:47.680
when you look at this project?

00:36:47.680 --> 00:36:49.430
And we'll get to some
numbers in a minute,

00:36:49.430 --> 00:36:50.760
but how do you measure success?

00:36:50.760 --> 00:36:53.613
How do you know if
this project succeeded?

00:36:53.613 --> 00:36:54.530
It's not reduced cost.

00:36:54.530 --> 00:36:56.790
Obviously mTLS, there's
one thing, but how else?

00:36:56.790 --> 00:36:58.520
Are there any metrics
you would use?

00:36:58.520 --> 00:37:00.560
RUSSELL WARMAN: So the
frequency of deployments

00:37:00.560 --> 00:37:02.540
is the big thing
for us, making sure

00:37:02.540 --> 00:37:06.380
that our development
teams can release more

00:37:06.380 --> 00:37:08.370
frequently and more safely.

00:37:08.370 --> 00:37:11.780
So not having customer
impact is absolutely

00:37:11.780 --> 00:37:13.250
the measures of success for us.

00:37:13.250 --> 00:37:14.792
DAN CIRULI: Frequently
of deployment,

00:37:14.792 --> 00:37:16.890
and the success of
those deployments.

00:37:16.890 --> 00:37:19.710
Conveniently, Russ
provided me with some data

00:37:19.710 --> 00:37:20.960
that we've thrown up here now.

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:22.900
You track the number of
deployments that you do.

00:37:22.900 --> 00:37:23.775
RUSSELL WARMAN: Yeah.

00:37:23.775 --> 00:37:27.560
So we've been tracking this,
as you can see, back to 2011,

00:37:27.560 --> 00:37:29.665
I think it is.

00:37:29.665 --> 00:37:31.040
This shows the
journey that we've

00:37:31.040 --> 00:37:33.890
been on from manual deployment
through to auto deploy

00:37:33.890 --> 00:37:38.820
and now as we've started
moving onto the new platform.

00:37:38.820 --> 00:37:40.790
So you can see the Karl
effect there in there.

00:37:40.790 --> 00:37:41.620
DAN CIRULI: The Karl effect.

00:37:41.620 --> 00:37:41.970
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:41.970 --> 00:37:42.845
RUSSELL WARMAN: Yeah.

00:37:42.845 --> 00:37:46.670
DAN CIRULI: I mean this is
an almost unbelievable graph,

00:37:46.670 --> 00:37:49.060
but I saw the numbers myself.

00:37:49.060 --> 00:37:51.230
So the rate at which
your developers

00:37:51.230 --> 00:37:54.700
can get code into production
and get a new feature out

00:37:54.700 --> 00:37:56.403
has increased dramatically.

00:37:56.403 --> 00:37:58.820
RUSSELL WARMAN: Yeah, and this
year we're predicting also,

00:37:58.820 --> 00:38:00.180
this coming financial year.

00:38:00.180 --> 00:38:01.790
We went from April to March.

00:38:01.790 --> 00:38:04.530
We're predicting that that
number is going to hit 30,000.

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:07.190
DAN CIRULI: So doubling
again after quadrupling

00:38:07.190 --> 00:38:09.380
in the last couple of years.

00:38:09.380 --> 00:38:11.008
How many of those are failing?

00:38:11.008 --> 00:38:13.550
RUSSELL WARMAN: Well you see
the numbers are just going down.

00:38:13.550 --> 00:38:16.410
Year on year we're
getting more confident

00:38:16.410 --> 00:38:21.620
that the platform is capable
of supporting more change,

00:38:21.620 --> 00:38:23.688
but it's not impacting
our customers at all.

00:38:23.688 --> 00:38:25.230
DAN CIRULI: This is
not a percentage.

00:38:25.230 --> 00:38:26.022
This is raw number.

00:38:26.022 --> 00:38:27.980
RUSSELL WARMAN: This is
absolutely raw numbers.

00:38:27.980 --> 00:38:30.200
DAN CIRULI: So as we saw
that number of deployments

00:38:30.200 --> 00:38:32.575
go way up, you've seen the
number of failing deployments.

00:38:32.575 --> 00:38:37.955
This increased visibility,
what do you attribute that?

00:38:37.955 --> 00:38:39.330
KARL STONEY: So,
I actually think

00:38:39.330 --> 00:38:41.730
the main driver behind
this is because it's

00:38:41.730 --> 00:38:42.930
so quick to deploy now.

00:38:42.930 --> 00:38:44.790
I mean, literally you
can go from a change

00:38:44.790 --> 00:38:46.080
into production in
a couple of minutes.

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:47.580
People are just
releasing more frequently

00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:49.890
and doing smaller changes,
whereas before if it takes

00:38:49.890 --> 00:38:52.050
say two, three hours
to do a release

00:38:52.050 --> 00:38:54.300
they'll just bundle up more.

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:56.983
And then those things go
wrong more, basically.

00:38:56.983 --> 00:38:59.400
DAN CIRULI: So when we look
on this on a percentage basis,

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:02.070
we've gone from four out
of every 100 deployments

00:39:02.070 --> 00:39:05.580
having a problem to two out
of every 1,000 deployments

00:39:05.580 --> 00:39:07.890
having a problem.

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:10.260
It's an impressive
thing that you've done.

00:39:10.260 --> 00:39:12.810
So the status today,
you're halfway through.

00:39:12.810 --> 00:39:15.750
You've got about half your
services running, all of them

00:39:15.750 --> 00:39:17.730
with secure traffic.

00:39:17.730 --> 00:39:20.160
You've reduced the
footprint for most of them.

00:39:20.160 --> 00:39:23.430
Obviously you're deploying
a lot very successfully,

00:39:23.430 --> 00:39:27.420
and we gave you a new
feature recently in 1.1,

00:39:27.420 --> 00:39:28.770
and you had to roll that out.

00:39:28.770 --> 00:39:30.328
How long did that take?

00:39:30.328 --> 00:39:32.370
KARL STONEY: So in order
to get that feature out,

00:39:32.370 --> 00:39:34.470
we had to redeploy
every application.

00:39:34.470 --> 00:39:36.450
I think it took two
of our engineers

00:39:36.450 --> 00:39:38.070
to implement the
feature and roll out

00:39:38.070 --> 00:39:39.780
every app about three hours.

00:39:39.780 --> 00:39:41.860
DAN CIRULI: So they changed
their CICD pipeline.

00:39:41.860 --> 00:39:42.350
KARL STONEY: Yes.

00:39:42.350 --> 00:39:44.250
We actually changed the
platform because the platform

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:45.600
is deployed in the
same way as, actually,

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:46.600
all of the applications.

00:39:46.600 --> 00:39:49.050
So change the platform,
implement the new Istio feature

00:39:49.050 --> 00:39:51.780
behind the abstraction, roll
out the applications in order

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:54.050
to pull in that new feature.

00:39:54.050 --> 00:39:55.140
Yes, very quick.

00:39:55.140 --> 00:39:56.490
DAN CIRULI: Yeah.

00:39:56.490 --> 00:39:58.547
So if you want to get
started with Istio,

00:39:58.547 --> 00:40:00.130
obviously it's an
open source project.

00:40:00.130 --> 00:40:03.720
You can go to istio.io, you
can install it yourself.

00:40:03.720 --> 00:40:08.130
On GKE we do have an
automated installer which

00:40:08.130 --> 00:40:12.360
will take open source Istio and
plop that right on to your--

00:40:12.360 --> 00:40:13.920
that's my animation
right there--

00:40:13.920 --> 00:40:16.360
plop that onto your GKE cluster.

00:40:16.360 --> 00:40:18.210
You can choose whether
or not you want mTLS.

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:21.450
It will automatically send
your metrics to Stackdriver.

00:40:21.450 --> 00:40:24.850
You can plug in your own
Mixer adapters as well.

00:40:24.850 --> 00:40:26.670
It's a great way to get started.

00:40:26.670 --> 00:40:31.330
We do have a lot of people
who are using it now.

00:40:31.330 --> 00:40:34.620
We not only do the installation,
but we will do upgrades for you

00:40:34.620 --> 00:40:35.140
as well.

00:40:35.140 --> 00:40:38.040
So when a new GKE
version comes out,

00:40:38.040 --> 00:40:39.540
if there's a new
version of Istio,

00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:44.010
we will automatically
upgrade that for you too.

00:40:44.010 --> 00:40:46.500
It's not necessarily for
every production deployment

00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:50.280
it does constrain which features
of Istio you can change.

00:40:50.280 --> 00:40:52.260
If you need more flexibility
than it gives you,

00:40:52.260 --> 00:40:56.400
it's not hard to install
Istio yourself as you learned.

00:40:56.400 --> 00:40:58.350
And of course with the
Anthos announcement

00:40:58.350 --> 00:41:01.350
yesterday, one of the
key components in Anthos

00:41:01.350 --> 00:41:03.360
is service match
so we are working

00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:06.720
on productized versions
of managed versions of all

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:07.780
the services as well.

00:41:07.780 --> 00:41:11.880
So Istio will be included,
or some brand name version

00:41:11.880 --> 00:41:13.968
of Istio, will be
included with Anthos.

00:41:13.968 --> 00:41:15.510
And as an open source
project, you're

00:41:15.510 --> 00:41:17.880
welcome to join the community.

00:41:17.880 --> 00:41:22.170
We have community meetings every
two weeks when people come in.

00:41:22.170 --> 00:41:24.960
And it's largely users, not
people who are contributing

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:26.730
to Istio, so I encourage that.

00:41:26.730 --> 00:41:30.120
So thank you very much
everyone, please do rate.

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:32.160
Thank you to the crew in
the back for your help.

00:41:32.160 --> 00:41:33.118
Have a good conference.

00:41:33.118 --> 00:41:36.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

